              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Classical mechanics is that part of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            dealing with the deterministic physics of point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particles
            and rigid bodies; often the systems with the infinitely many degrees of freedom are also included (like infinite arrays of particles and their continuous limits like classical mechanics of strings, membranes, elastic media and of classical fields). For the continuous systems, the equations of motion can often be explained by the partial differential equations, describing classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+field"} physical fields
            of quantities (typically smooth possibly vector valued functions on manifolds), including background fields like metric; the latter (sub)area is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
          %p
            Nondissipative systems with finitely many degrees of freedom may be described geometrically using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
          %p
            Classical mechanics of a system of point particles and rigid bodies is usually divided into statics, kinematics and dynamics.
            %strong Statics
            studies the balance of forces in a system which does not move, or in a stationary flow.
            %strong Kinematics
            studies the relation between position, velocity and acceleration of bodies in a mechanical system, without reference to the causes of motion.
            %strong Dynamics
            studies motion with reference to the causes of motion and interaction between bodies and its manifestation via (quantified)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} forces
          %p
            For a theoretical classical mechanics one often starts with a concrete system of bodies with pulleys, strings, spins, external and internal forces, and dissipative sinks and sources (e.g. friction forces), which are then analysed to get the configuration or phase space of the system, the equations of motion and possibly to determine some special
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observables
            of interest. Once abstracted that way, the rest of the study is a rather special case of the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+system"} dynamical systems
          %p
            A terminological and scope discussion is archived
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/comments.php?DiscussionID=2807&Focus=23543#Comment_23543"} here
          %h2#traditional_formulation Traditional formulation
          %p The fundamental distinction is between open and closed mechanical systems. Open systems have exchange of energy with the rest of universe, that is with the energy sources or sinks which are not described by the mechanical system: for example the energy can be absorbed through forces from external bodies not belonging to the system but accounted in terms of such forces, or the energy can be lost by heating (described by friction force and alike). Closed systems are conservative in energetic sense. The terminology open/closed is wider than conservative/nonconservative, as it pertains also to statistical and quantum systems.
          %p The standard formalisms for classical mechanics are Newtonian mechanics, Lagrangean formalism, and the Hamiltonian formalism which can be studied in the generality of symplectic manifolds and more general (allowing degeneracies) formalism of Poisson manifolds; Poisson manifolds can generalize to he study of somewhat nonclassically to (the opposite of) more general Poisson algebras.
          %h3#newtonian_formalism Newtonian formalism
          %p …
          %h3#lagrangean_formalism Lagrangean formalism
          %p …
          %h3#hamiltonian_formalism_and_symplectic_manifolds Hamiltonian formalism and symplectic manifolds
          %p
            (…)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            (…)
          %h3#mechanical_systems_based_on_poisson_algebras Mechanical systems based on Poisson algebras
          %p We set up some basic notions of classical mechanics.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                real
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
              is a unital (commutative)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ˙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \dot)
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real numbers
              that is equipped with with an additional bilinear operation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [-,-] : A \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} A \to A
            %p
              that makes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              into a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              such that for each element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              the operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,-] : A \to A
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
              of the product
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot : A \otimes A \to A
            %p
              This definition readily generalized to symmetric monoidal categories. For example, a real
              %em super Poisson algebra
              is a graded-commutative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
              equipped with the compatible structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ˙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \dot, [-,-]) \to (B, \cdot, [-,-])
              of (super) Poisson algebras is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              that respects both the associative product and the Lie bracket.
            %p
              Write
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poiss"} Poiss
              for the resulting category of (super) Poisson algebras.
          %p The standard setup of conservative classical mechanical system is a Poisson manifold.
          #PoissonManifoldExample.num_defn
            %h6#definition__principal_example Definition - principal example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              is a smooth real manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              equipped with a Poisson structure (that is the Poisson bracket) on the commutative algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(M)
              of smooth real valued functions on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            Recall that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            provides an example of a Poisson manifold. Possibly infinite-dimensional generalization of this example is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
          #ClassMechSys.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              of that of commutative real (super) Poisson algebras we call the category of
              %strong classical mechanical systems
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ClassMechSys
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi CPoiss
                      %mi op
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ClassMechSys \coloneqq CPoiss^{op}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Poisson superalgebras describe systems with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermions
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              This definition captures most notions of “mechanical systems”. Exceptions contain
              %span.newWikiWord
                open systems
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/open+system"} ?
              for which there is no conservation laws (examples: externally-driven and
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dissipative+system"} dissipative systems
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \cdot, \{-,-\})
              a Poisson algebra,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              together with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^1(A)
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differentials
              naturally form a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Rinehart+pair"} Lie-Rinehart pair
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi d
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [d a, d b ] \coloneqq d \{a,b\}
                    \\,.
            %p
              If the Poisson algebra comes from a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
              of the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h3#observables_and_states Observables and states
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \coloneqq (A,\cdot, \{-,-\})
              a classical mechanical system, we say
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  is an element
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong algebra of observables
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  is
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      a
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi ρ
                              %mo :
                              %mi A
                              %mo →
                              %mi ℝ
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{R}
                    %ul
                      %li
                        %p
                          which is
                          %strong positive
                          in that for all
                          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                            %semantics
                              %mrow
                                %mi a
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi A
                              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
                          we have that
                        %p
                          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                            %semantics
                              %mrow
                                %mi ρ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi a
                                %mo ⋅
                                %mi a
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ≥
                                %mn 0
                              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a \cdot a) \geq 0
                          ;
                      %li
                        %p
                          and which is
                          %strong normalized
                          in that
                          = succeed "." do
                            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                              %semantics
                                %mrow
                                  %mi ρ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo =
                                  %mn 1
                                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(1) = 1
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  is a state that is not only a linear map, but even an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ρ
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℝ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{R}
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi States
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} States((A, \cdot))
              for the set of states of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi States
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \in States
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a)
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  value of the observable
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                  on the system in state
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              If the classical mechanical system comes from a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
              by example
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PoissonManifoldExample"}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is one
              %em specific
              (= “pure”) state that the mechanical system defined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
              can be in, whereas a general state
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{R}
              is a distribution of such specific states.
          %h3#flows_and_evolution Flows and evolution
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = C^\infty(M)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be a Poisson algebra with a good notion of weak topology and corresponding notion of a derivative.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              For any observable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              we say that a
              %strong 1-parameter flow induced by the observable
              is, if it exists, an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real numbers
              on the observables
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_a : \mathbb{R} \times A \to A
            %p
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiable
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              and satisfies for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in A
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi t
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{d}{d t} F_a(b) (t) = \{a,b\}
                    \\,.
            %p
              with initial value
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_a(b)(0) = b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              (pointwise we get to differentiate a function
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}
          %p
            In non-relativistic classical mechanics every system comes up with a choice of one element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in A
            and declare that the corresponding flow is the
            %em time evolution
            of observables of the system. One calls this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            or
            %em energy observable
            of the system. The physical meaning, however, does not change if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is changed by an overall constant.
          %h3#quantization Quantization
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi StarAlg
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} StarAlg_{\mathbb{C}}
              for the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebras
              over the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex numbers
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi QuantMechSys
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi StarAlg
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    QuantMechSys \coloneqq StarAlg^{op}
            %p
              we call the category of
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+systems"} quantum mechanical systems
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \cdot, \{-,-\})
              a classical mechanical system, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassMechSys"}
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              of it is – if it exists –
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a 1-parameter
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    field of star-algebras
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/field+of+star-algebras"} ?
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mi ℏ
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mrow
                              %mi ℏ
                              %mo ∈
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mn 0
                              %mo ,
                              %mn ∞
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{A_\hbar\}_{\hbar \in [0,\infty)}
              %li
                %p
                  such that in the limit
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar \to 0
                  we have
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi A
                              %mi ℏ
                            %mo →
                            %msub
                              %mi A
                              %mn 0
                            %mo ≔
                            %mi A
                            %msub
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi ℝ
                            %mi ℂ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\hbar \to A_0 \coloneqq A \otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}
                      ;
                  %li
                    %p
                      for all
                      = succeed ":" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ∈
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mi ℏ
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in A_{\hbar}
                      = succeed "." do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mfrac
                                %mn 1
                                %mrow
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi ℏ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{i \hbar } [a,b] = \{a,b\}
            %p
              Conversely, given a quantum mechanical system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \ast)
              and a field of star-algebras such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = A_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
              its (or rather: a)
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+limit"} classical limit
              \.
          %h2#formulation_in_higher_geometry_by_local_prequantum_field_theory Formulation in higher geometry by local prequantum field theory
          %p
            Traditional classical mechanics (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"}> Hamiltonian mechanics
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+mechanics"} Lagrangian mechanics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton-Jacobi+theory"} Hamilton-Jacobi theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            formulated in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondence"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondence
              \.
          %h2#particular_classical_systems Particular classical systems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+dynamics+on+Lie+groups"} Hamiltonian dynamics on Lie groups
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+body+dynamics"} rigid body dynamics
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"}> mechanics
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contact+manifold"} contact manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} semiclassical approximation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+anomaly"} classical anomaly
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"}> quantization
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"}> quantum mechanics
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"}> quantum field theory
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+function+in+classical+mechanics"}> generating function in classical mechanics
                ,
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The origin of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton%27s+laws+of+motion"} Newton's laws of motion
          %ul
            %li#Newton1687
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isaac+Newton"}> Isaac Newton
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philosophi%C3%A6+naturalis+principia+mathematica"} Philosophiæ naturalis principia mathematica
              (1687)
          %p Classical textbooks:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+W.+B.+Kibble"}> Tom W. B. Kibble
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Berkshire"} Frank Berkshire
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classical mechanics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://physicaeducator.files.wordpress.com/2018/02/classical-mechanics-by-kibble-and-berkshire.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
